"Duncan Booth" <me@privacy.net> wrote:

> Simply dropping an unknown box gives you a total
> price (the shopkeeper obviously looked inside),
> but doesn't give you a detailed inventory.

Well, that's the problem: when you _pick up_ the box
you just get a total price, but if you buy it and
then *drop* it to sell it back, you _do_ get an
inventory as a side benefit.  Since this pick-up,
buy, drop, sell cycle is exactly the way you turn
cloned credit into hard cash, the adventurer is
getting a free peek into the container's contents,
without taking the risk of traps or having the tools
to open it without damaging the contents.

I don't think that's appropriate. It makes credit
cloning even more unbalancing than it already was.

I'd prefer that any unknown contents of a container
sell either as an unidentified lot, or not at all,
but not be broken out even by cost, as now, much
less be named to the player.

This might well be a mess to implement; does each
object already have an "ever seen" flag, or would
one need adding to the code? If the latter, it
might still be worth the candle, since several
game artifacts are due to the player being given
info s/he can't possibly know at the time about
objects.

xanthian.

Again, I've only tried this with ice chests and bags,
I don't know, just assume, that the same thing will
happen with chests and large boxes.


